On Tue, 11 Aug 1998, Christoph Nadig wrote:

> Keith Baker wrote:
> 
> > Well, I would like to know how to do it anyhow...  I'd like to know what I
> > am doing wrong...  I think it has to do with chunk size...  What is the
> > "chunk size factor"...  all I know is that I have 4k Chunks.  I understand
> > what a chunk is, just not what units the "chunk factor" is in...  I'd like
> > to partition my drive more, but I allways run out of space on one
> > partition way before the others.  What partitions have relitively static
> > sizes which can be sized based on what they are now.  I would guess
> > something like /lib doesn't grow all that much where as /opt and /usr seem
> > to grow as most new files go in there...
> 
> 4k Chunks are not possible on Alphas, since a chunk is a multipleoif a memory
> page which are 8k for Alphas. The chunk size factor tells
> by how many bits the kernel should shift the memory page size to get
> the actual chunk size. So for 8k chunks the chunk size factor is
> 0, for 16k chunks it's 1 and so on.
> 
