You could do what I do. Give the OS it's own partition, around
1 GB and then keep your user home directories and everything
else important some other place. That way, you don't
have to figure which part of the system ran out of space. Also
add a swap space if you need one. I don't use them on my Linux
boxes. I'd rather a process died than run with swapping.

I usually keep everything but the OS on a file server and NFS
mount it to all the other systems I use. Then, what space is
left locally, I use for scratch space. As always, YMMV.

I'm sure lots of other folks have their own preferred method
for dividing up disk but I'm in the Keep It Simple category.

One thing though, I do use 100Mb switched Enet for all my
boxes so NFS isn't much of a problem.


Wes


