With regards to your message at 10:43 PM 08-11-98 +0000, dca2. Where you
stated:
>Hi again,
>
>I have decided to go with SCSI instead of IDE.  I have not used SCSI
>before.. I wanted the 2940UW but settled for a Diamond Fireport 40. I
>also picked up (2) - 1G IBM hard drives without documentation. Any one
>with knowleged as to where to find info would be appreciated. I am
>searching IBM now.
>
>Also, I got some conflicting statements today about SCSI configuration.
>The manual tells me to set boot drive to ID 0. The guy at the computer
>store said the higher the number the higher the priority. What is best
>the best solution? I still have to figure out how these are jumpered
>too.

The guy at the store is "full of it".
In a DOS/Windows setup you probably want to make the "C" drive on 0, or at
least the lowest ID. In Linux it doesn't matter. YOU are telling the system
where to boot from with something like boot "sda1:vmlinux.gz".
Some conventions apply here:
On most UNIX families the first 4 IDs (0 to 3) are reserved for hard disks,
while the CDROM is most often at 4, and tape and otherdevices occupy from 5
up.
With wide SCSI you have 15 devices available, but remember, only wide
devices can have IDs over #6.
The controller is usually ( by default) #7.
The lowest ID hard drive found become sda, the nesxt sdb, and so on. The
lowest ID CDROM found is scd0, the next one scd1, etc.
Personally I like to make the first drive #1 ( not 0) so that if I want to
plug in another hard drive and boot from it, or to try an experimental
install, I can make this "temporary" drive ID 0, and it becomes sda.

If you have an AlphaBIOS based system you see a message when the SCSI card
fires up on bootup which says "Press Control-C to configure" 
It is good on an UltraWide card like the Diamond to take the time to
configure your device selections. 8 bit for narrow drives, 16 bit for wide.
20MHz for UltraSCSI, 10MHz for SCSI-2 fast drives.
LUN support is not normally needed, nor is it necessary to scan the bus for
any drive but the one you are loading linload.exe, milo, etc from..


