On Wed, 12 Aug 1998, David Alan Gilbert wrote:
> Roman Martonak wrote:
> > I have connected an HP Laserjet 2Pplus printer to my UDB and it turns out that
> > I cannot print postscript files, although ASCII files are printed without any
> > problem. When I try to print even a small (10 kB) postscript file, the printer
> > reports ERROR 20, which means insufficient printer memory. I have configured
> > the printer using the RedHat printtool from the control panel. Do I really need
> > to extend the memory of the printer or is the problem somewhere else and could
> > be solved in a simple way ? The printer does not have a postscript card, but as
> > I understood from Printing - HOWTO, it should not be necessary, since
> > postscript should be interpreted by ghostcript, am I right?
> 
> Ghostscript does indeed interpret the postscript; but the problem is that it
> then sends
> the image to the printer as a bitmap and thus your printer needs enough memory
> to hold that bitmap.
> At 300x300 dpi on an A4/Letter sheet you need just under 1MB of RAM - no matter
> how simple the postscript.

And most older printers with 1 MB of memory won't grok a full A4. Newer
printers keep a compressed image in memory, so they need less memory.

Solution: tell ghostscript to render in a lower resolution (150 dpi or 200
dpi).

Greetings,

						Geert

