On 13-Aug-98 Kenneth J Baker wrote:
> Is there a chance someone would be willing to summarize installing KDE 1.0
> on an Alpha with Redhat 5.1 installed on it?  

Get the source for qt-1.33 from www.troll.no. Get the source for EGCS 1.0.3a
from Cygnus. Get the source for all (9) KDE packages from ftp.kde.org.

Unpack each tarball into its own separate directory. Perform "./configure",
"make" and "make install" on each package in the following order:

EGCS
QT
kdesupport
kdelibs
kdebase
all the rest of the kde packages in any order.

Everything should compile with no errors (warnings, yes, but not errors). If
you do get an error figure out why before proceding.

Add the path to your kde executables to your path (usually /usr/local/kde but
yours might be different)

Create a .xinitrc file in your home directory with the single line "startkde".

Type "startx" and KDE should come up.

This procedure worked for me. I may have left out a step or two since it's been
a while. As always be sure to back up anything critical. I take no
responsibility for the accuracy of this procedure.

I recall that one or two of the packages would not compile unless
/usr/src/linux was linked to the original RedHat source tree, it failed when
linked to pristine sources patched with the gatekeeper patches.

Also, if you are running an SX164 machine, you will have to edit config.sub in
each source tree. Change the (2) references to alphaev56 to alphapca56. There
may be other machines that autoconfigure does not properly recognize.

I also had two instances where the machine was not correctly recgnized by
autoconf. Re-running ./configure again (after deleting config.cache) solved the
problem in both cases.

The entire procedure took about 3 hours on a 533Mhz. machine with 192Mb. RAM (a
lot longer on a UDB). Your mileage may vary. If you run into a problem feel free
to send me mail, I've set this up on 5 different Alpha machines running RH 5.0
and 5.1.

---
Brian E.W. Wood  beww@intac.com


