With regards to your message at 04:37 PM 08-13-98 +0000, dca2. Where you
stated:
>If all goes well I'll be installing software tomorrow or this weekend. I
>will be installing both WinNT 4.0 and RedHat Linux. My focus is
>defiantely on Linux, not NT. But I would like to emulate my system on
>the Intel platform. I have NT here and Linux is to be shipped and arrive
>Friday.
>
>Any thoughts about set up would be appreciated. Install different OS's
>to different HD's? Should I Split the first HD and install both leaving
>ID1 open for data?
>
>Thanks for info
>Jeff
For NT and Linux it's a snap.
Install NT first. Go in the NT disk and run the ARCINST.EXE program from
the /alpha directory on the CDROM, and partition the HD with a FAT
partition at the brginning of the first disk. I suggest about 300MB to hold
the OS.
Then go ahead and install NT there.
Next, partition the disk(s) from NT, but leave the amount of space you want
for the Linux partitions, at least 200MB for Linux root (/) at least the
same amount of swap as RAM, up to 128MB. And as much as you want for /USR
on another partition.
  You can only create 4 primary partitions on a disk, but you may create an
extended partition which may contain many logical partitions.

Finally, install Linux, and create the different boot options in the ARC
menu for boot entries. Remeber in the command options to start Linux use
the form:
boot sda2:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2
In this example we assume that the root partition is on the first SCSI HD
(sda) and in the 2nd partition (sda2). Change to suit your configuration.
Finally, copy the linload.exe and milo files to the first (FAT16) partition
you installed NT onto. Tell the menu entry in the ARC loader options to
find linload.exe and milo there.
It's easy, and you do NOT need to sepaerate this on 2 disks. Of course you
may, if ytou want to..


