I have noticed a considerable decline in floating-point performance in
upgrading from Redhat4.2 (for alpha) to Redhat 5.1 (same alpha).  As an
example, the spec.c code below
executes in 27. seconds (time output) when run on my 433MHz RH5.1 alpha
when compiled as "gcc spec.c -o spec -lm" while it runs in 4.3 seconds
on
the same machine if I link to the old (from RH4.2) math library,
-lm-1.99,
(though the compile gives me warnings).  I have tried a variety of
compiler
flags when linking with the new math library but have not improved
performance.  I gather RH5.1 has a new glibc package and was wondering
if I
have to live with this huge performance hit.  Is there a patch or flag I
am
missing?  Has anyone else had problems?

#include <math.h>

int main()
{
  int i, j;
  double ftemp;

  ftemp=1.;
  for(i=1;i<10000;i++) {
    for(j=1;j<1000;j++) {
      ftemp+= 2.*sqrt(ftemp);
      ftemp*=0.5;
    }
  }
  printf("%g\n",ftemp);
}



