> For NT and Linux it's a snap.
> Install NT first. Go in the NT disk and run the ARCINST.EXE program
> from
> the /alpha directory on the CDROM, and partition the HD with a FAT
> partition at the brginning of the first disk. I suggest about 300MB to
> hold
> the OS.
> Then go ahead and install NT there.
> Next, partition the disk(s) from NT, but leave the amount of space you
> want
> for the Linux partitions, at least 200MB for Linux root (/) at least
> the
> same amount of swap as RAM, up to 128MB. And as much as you want for
> /USR
> on another partition.
>   You can only create 4 primary partitions on a disk, but you may
> create an
> extended partition which may contain many logical partitions.
> 
> Finally, install Linux, and create the different boot options in the
> ARC
> menu for boot entries. Remeber in the command options to start Linux
> use
> the form:
> boot sda2:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2
> In this example we assume that the root partition is on the first SCSI
> HD
> (sda) and in the 2nd partition (sda2). Change to suit your
> configuration.
> Finally, copy the linload.exe and milo files to the first (FAT16)
> partition
> you installed NT onto. Tell the menu entry in the ARC loader options
> to
> find linload.exe and milo there.
> It's easy, and you do NOT need to sepaerate this on 2 disks. Of course
> you
> may, if ytou want to..
