With regards to your message at 12:27 AM 08-15-98 +0000, dca2. Where you
stated:
>> Calvin,
>
>I will check when I can get in to bios of the card. The Fireport book says
it's
>auto terminating only when it needs to be. ) I will reread this section to
be sure)
>I do have however, 4 sets of pins on this card I have yet to identify in the
>manual. They are open - no jumpers.
>
>> The one other thing I would check is to get into the bios setup of the SCSI
>> card and make sure it is not trying to terminate one end of the bus at the
>> card.  In other words turn off all termination at the card, since it
sits in the
>> middle of your bus.

This is COMPLETELY WRONG.
1) The card has autotermination on it, and IT WORKS.
Leave it alone. I have installed these with many numbers and types of SCSI
devices, and the auto-termination works flawlessly.

2) In the case of his machine he has stated that he has a narrow SCSI cable
connecting to his 8-bit SCSI CDROM drive. He is leaving termination "on" in
this drive.
He also has a wide SCSI cable going from the card to his 16-bit SCSI hard
drives, with the one on the far end with its' termination ON.
So, he actually needs the card to provide termination on the high half of
the 16-bit bus, and none on the low half.

Diagram ( bad ASCII art) :

           ______________           ________________          _____________
           |             |          |               |         |            |
high byte->|16-bit TERM =|==========|== TERM  ======|         |            |
low byte ->|16-bit TERM =|==========|== NO TERM=====|=========|=TERM 8-bit |
           |             |          |               |         |            |
           ---------------          -----------------         --------------
           16 bit SCSI HD         16 bit SCSI controller     8 bit SCSI CDROM
The low byte of the SCSI bus is terminated at the CDROM and at the HD at
the end of the bus.
The high byte is terminated at the SCSI card and at the controller card.



>I have taken the box apart..checked all settings..and rearranged the cables.

Good luck!

