On Fri, 14 Aug 1998, B. James Phillippe wrote:

> 	Trying to port some Intel code to the Alpha.  The code includes
> <sys/io.h> on Intel/glibc-2.0 and uses inb() and outb() without problems.
> Looks like on Alpha these are macros defined to use true functions _outb()
> and _inb().  My program won't link due to unresolved symbols and I can't
> seem to find any file containing these functions anywhere on my disk.  I
> have looked in linux-2.0.35 and linux-2.1.115.  My system is Avanti (Apecs)
> running RedHat-5.1 w/glibc-2.0.

 For glibc 2.0.7 _outb() is a function exported from the library as is
outb() (one of them is an alias to the other, AFAIR).  Just do `objdump -T
/lib/libc-2.0.7.so' and search for it. 

 For glibc 2.0.94 there is only outb() exported from the library so you
have to "#undef outb" after including <sys/io.h>.

 You may want to explore the SVGATextMode package as it uses direct I/O
and works on Alpha (and is somewhat less complicated than XFree86).

 It may be also quite interesting to see how 'ioperm()' and I/O functions
are actually implemented on Alpha. 

