Armin Ollig <armin@data.tops.net> wrote:
> The NT Posix "subsystem" (a hack iam shure like the rest of NT) is
> almost impossible to "exploit". MS will not make any docs about it
> publicly available.

The NT Posix subsytem doesn't need any special docs from MS.  It implements
*exactly* Posix 1.  No more, no less.  It was developed to provide the
minimal degree of compliance with the Posix spec necessary in order be
elgible for US government procurement.

So if you want to develop for the Posix subsystem, there's really nothing
to it.  The header files are provided with Visual C++, and you just have to
give an option to the linker to get it to build the right kind of executable.

On the other hand, there's really no reason to develop for the Posix
subsystem (on Alpha or x86).  You can't actually do anything interesting
with it.  For instance, there are no sockets, because sockets aren't part
of Posix 1.

What would be much more interesting than the NT Posix subsystem would be
an NT Linux subsystem (sort of like the Linux/Mach for the PowerPC), or
better yet, an NT subsystem under Linux.  I've actually looked into doing
the latter.  The main technical difficulty is that in order to fully
virtualize the Alpha architecture, you would need PALcode that supports
virtualizing PAL calls.


