I used "tar -cvf /dev/st0 ." in the appropriate directory
so it never touches the file system on output.

It surprises me that tar has the problem because the same
problem would exist on Cray's and under DEC UNIX so it should
have been sorted out a while back.

A while back I ran into a Fujitsu UNIX mainframe that
had a weird tar format. It could be cpio can use the
newest tar formats and tar doesn't.

cpio man page says it can do:

              bin    The obsolete binary format.

              odc    The old (POSIX.1) portable format.

              newc   The  new  (SVR4) portable format, which sup-
                     ports file systems having more than 65536 i-
                     nodes.

              crc    The new (SVR4) portable format with a check-
                     sum added.

              tar    The old tar format.

              ustar  The POSIX.1 tar format.  Also recognizes GNU
                     tar  archives,  which  are  similar  but not
                     identical.

I'll try cpio though and see what it can do with newc and ustar.


Wes


