It isn't that bad.

Assume RH5.1 CDROM, the manuals, a milo off gatekeeper for an LX
(2.0.30),
an lx164.gz image floppy, and a ramdisk.img floppy.  Also requisite
hardware:

  An LX board with a working fail safe booter in sector 0

  Enough RAM. (I'd say at least 64M)

  a mouse

  a keyboard

  a VGA compliant card (to get the kernel loaded, for X you'll need a
targetted
 
        video card, I use a Millenium Matrox II.

  a hard drive (I'm using a Adaptec 2940UW SCSI card and an 8.6 G
Barracuda).

  a CDROM (I'm using a SCSI-2 on the 2940UW)  The 2940UW auto
terminates,
        the hard drive and CDROM are on arms of a tee, one with 16 bit
termination
        the other with 8 bit termination (on the CDROM).

  a floppy drive

  Pull CF7 with the milo floppy in the drive.  This gets you up in MILO
  which can operate either via the keyboard and VGA display or via
serial port A. 
  the small black book on Alphas with the CDROM has the semantics for
booting
  the kernel from MILO.  Note you could execute a 'show' command to see
that
  all your installed devices show up.  This in no guarantee that the
SCSI won't
  fail, you can do an 'ls' command under MILO.

  MILO> boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

   The last two parts tell your kernel to use the ramdisk floppy and
prompt
   for it.

   I installed the complete LINUX (you should look for updates on
redhat.com)
