AHA!  Finally went through drivers/block/Config.in and discovered that
for the CMD646 driver to be enabled, I needed to set
CONFIG_EXPERIMENTAL.  So, here is Adam's big list of things to do to get
IDE DMA working on Linux-Alpha:

1. Check arch/alpha/config.in to make sure your architecture sets
CONFIG_PCI.  (As far as I can tell, Jensen is the only one that
doesn't.)

2. (from Dave Gilbert) Go in and edit drivers/block/Config.in and just
under the line about NS87415 support add:

  bool '     CMD646 chipset support (EXPERIMENTAL)'
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_CMD646

3. Re-configure the kernel, making sure to:
 - Answer 'y' to "Prompt for development and/or incomplete
code/drivers", which should be the first question.
 - Answer 'y' to "Enhanced IDE/MFM/RLL disk/cdrom/tape/floppy support".
 - Answer 'y' to "Generic PCI IDE chipset support", "Generic PCI
bus-master DMA support", and "Use DMA by default when available".
 - Finally, answer 'y' to "CMD646 chipset support".

4. make dep; make clean; make boot; make modules; make modules_install
(though probably not all on one line)

5. Reboot, and "sit back" (Dave Gilbert again)!

On a dd time test, I get about 1/4 the clock time, and about 1/15 the
CPU time of my old non-DMA kernel.  As they say in Jamaica, "Zeeen!"

Corrections?  Comments?  Flames?  ("Adam, you should have known all that
from the beginning!")

Zeen,

