Right now the fastest "factory certified" speed you can get is 667MHz on a
21164A CPU, in a UX board. I understand that Microway have done some kind
of modified LX and run 667 CPUs on that..
The extra cost (over a 533) for this 667MHz CPU should be in the region of
$3600.
For a 633 CPU it should be about $1575 more than a 533.

As a note to this, the 533 CPU is discontinued, although there are still
quite a few in the distribution channel.

Samsung has also discontinued the LX board, as it competes with it's own UX
board, I guess..

Compaq still makes the LX, although due to some price increases from Intel
( who fabs Compaqs' CPUs now) the LX and CPU bundle from them is now VERY
expensive..

I have seen some articles claiming that Compaq has cut funding on some
co-op advertising on Alpha, but nothing definitive proves this. In the
article it quotes people from a few companies who are, or were "players" in
the Alpha clone business, but what isn't mentioned, IMHO, is the fact that
while the Alpha NT business seems to be fairly weak due to the newer Intel
350 and 400MHz offerings, it seems (at least from what we are seeing here)
to be getting stronger every day in the Linux and UNIX markets.


It's kind of like a soap opera right now, and hopefully the dust will
settle down soon..
I think most if it is fueled by everyone trying to second-guess what Compaq
is planning to do to market the Alpha, and until that is clearly defined we
are going to see a lot of smoke, but little substance...


Cheers,

