  Actually,
   As Steve Schweda so graciously pointed out, you will want to do your
fdisk first, leaving an
offset (I used one) of a cylinder or two at the base of your disk empty.
    Then, after  *carefully* noting the exact partition locations you chose
in fdisk, complete the RH
installation. After you manage to get it rebooted, (using, I presume,
something similar to the following: )
   "boot dkaX00 -file 1/vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/fd0" (where X is the
disk id, and '1' is the slice)

 run 'dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda bs=512', and then rerun fdisk, choosing
'b' for bsd style partitioning. Now, set up your bsd partitions to match
the partitions you chose within the previous
fdisk (for instance, I used '1' for root, '2' for swap, '3' for /usr, and
'4' for /home, so they mapped
the set as /dev/sda1 (a) for /, /dev/sda2 (b) for swap, /dev/sda3 (c) for
/usr, and /dev/sda4 (d) for
/home).

  You may now use 'swriteboot' to install your boot loader.

   I've actually just about finished an AS200 RH install URL  for  those
who insist on  using SRM to
boot/install. Unfortunately for the novice Alpha Linux hack (ok, I confess,
up until now I've been
a perfectly happy intel/Linux droid), the HOWTO's seem to be biased towards
MILO methodology...

   I'll post to the list and send email to strycher (who's url _is_
extremely helpful!) jic he wants to
point at it (and giggle :-))

Rob


