>Sounds like you made the mistake that I made once -- you firewalled out
>your nameserver :-).
>The delays you mention are programs trying to look up your nameserver. 
>Won't work unless you put in its IP address with an "allow" rule :-). 

Thank you for you're input, but I know how ipfwadm works.  The system does
not respond correctly with any deny rules.  If any rules set that allow
packets to be passed/accepted nothing goes wrong, until a deny policy is in
place.

This is what I started to test with, and everything works fine:  
ipfwadm -I -f
ipfwadm -I -p accept
ipfwadm -F -f
ipfwadm -O -f
(and to make you happy about dns, yet not needed)
ipfwadm -I -a accept -b -P udp -S 0.0.0.0/0 1024:65535 -D 0.0.0.0/0 53
ipfwadm -I -a accept -b -P udp -S 0.0.0.0/0 53 -D 0.0.0.0/0 1024:65535

The second I add any rule that denies any type of packet, the machine is
almost unusable.  If I were to deny icmp, the machine will just sit for
moments of time, and then go back into normal operation modes.  

As you were thinking that I was not allowing udp traffic on port 53; while
the system is under this serious lag, nslookups work fine.  The second any
deny rules are removed, the machine will react correctly once again.

I'm really not sure if its just a mistake I am making, or if I really do
have a problem.

-Barrett

