With regards to your message at 10:30 PM 08-20-98 +0200, C. Le Cannellier,
Pdt LUG Normandie. Where you stated:
>armin wrote :
>
>>Hi,
>Bonjour ;-)
>>i know this is a Linux list, but....:
>that's quite ok with me ;-)
>>i recently mamaged to get hold of a DU4d Licence for not-so-much money
>>from my university. Ofcause i want to install the DEC unix to use the nice
>>compiler and stuff. I have a couple of questions:
>great ! however, which compiler do you plan to use ? cxx (C++) and fortran
are 
>licensed thru a separate license ...
>>1. I cannot mount or read the CDROM from any Windows or Linux box
>>   The University dude told me the CDROM has a ufs Filesystem. really?
>no, this cdrom is cdfs type, you should be able to mount it
>what command do you use ?

The Digital UNIX Install disk is Digital UFS format. It is a bootable
filesystem on a CDROM. Digital UNIX can boot from UFS of AdVfs

>>2. All my PCI Cards seem to be pretty useless with DU
>>   The 2940 SCSI, the 3com 905 and the S3Virge cards do not work in SRM or
>>   DU. What cards would you recommend as a replacemant for DU and Linux ?
>dunno ... the only board I use for a mixed DU/Linux system is a DEC ZLXP-E1
>I believe that the Adaptec board would work, no idea for the others ...

You need a SCSI card with proper support for Digital UNIX, same for
ethernet. I recommend the IntraServer 4140UE - UNIX for this. You need to
buy the UNIX version to get the optimized drivers for UNIX. You can use the
NT/Linux version of this board, but it will reject the optimized drivers (
different ROM). 
The 4140UE has onboard 10/100T ethernet, using DEC chip and it works properly.
Generic tulip ethernet cards don't work.

For video the 3D10 is the cheapest solution. Basically it's a Number9 S3
Trio64 with a secial ROM. Only 2MB, so nothing very spectacular. Better is
MUCH more expensive..

>>2. The University dude told me i would need a DEC CDrom drive in order to
>>   install it. Is that true, do i really need a DEC SCSI Drive, or can i
>>   install it from any SCSI CDrom Drive ?
>you can install from *any* scsi cdrom drive (in any way, DEC drive areen't 
>manufactured by DEC but rather by Toshiba or others ...
>>best regards,

No, some DON't work properly. Even in the Toshibas certain ROM revisions
don't work right. For example, I once used a Toshiba 24X that wrked,
sort-of. But it wouldn't read certain disks. Drive worked fine in Linux and
NT.
Most SCSI CDROM drives work, not ALL. I have had good experience with
Toshiba and Sony.

>regards,
>
>Christophe


Cheers,

