mgraffam@mhv.net said:
> www.memory4less.com has 16x36 simms for $165 a piece. 128m simms are
> $300 a piece. At that price, compared to those you quote, it makes
> sense to get two 128m simms, and keep some of what you got.

It is a PC164-500MHZ. There are 8 SIMM slots that must be filled 4 at a
time. All the filled slots must have simms with the same dimensions.

If I fill only 4 slots, I get a 128bit wide memory path, if I fill all
8 a 256bit wide memory path. Since the simms are accessed in parallel
(this is good:-) they must be the same size (this is bad:-()

The PC164 only supports 512MB, so using 128M simms would limit me to 4,
and hence to a narrow memory path. A waste. So, a fully stuffed PC164
is 512MB as 8 64MB simms and a 256bit memory bus.

Got that?

My alpha is wonderful -- its fast, it's reliable, it's affordable, it's
running linux and is just a touch counter-culture. This memory business
is a bit of a bummer, though.
