With regards to your message at 02:08 PM 08-21-98 +1200, Chris Wedgwood.
Where you stated:
>I have a noname with 64MB of ECC/Parity (I don't know which) memory.
>
>Anyhow, is the ECC/parity actually used on this hardware?

Yes, the ONLY Alpha board you could get that doesn't need/use parity RAM is
the PC164SX.
I don't highly recommend it because it uses the AlphaPC chip without
onboard L2 cache (normally 96K) This cuts it's performance versus the
21164A at the same clock by about 40%. More on floating point intensive stuff.

The first PC164's used SIMMs and the ALCOR chipset, not bad, but not as
fast as the new boards with PYXIS and SDRAM DIMMs. It uses 2 banks of 4
SIMMs. If you fill one bank you get a 128 bit memory path, and with both
filled ( must be identical size SIMMs) you get 256 bit.

>I ask this because I'm getting sig11's with egcs in a fairly
>inconsistent manner (hard to reproduce, but every 5 kernel builds or
>so I'll see it), only I'm not seeing an parity error violations, etc.
>which I would expect assuming parity checking is implemented in the
>silicon and the OS knows about it...

Yes, but it may be happening enought times to cause an overflow failure.
The noname also has notoriously bad memory bandwidth. They cut costs on the
noname and UDB by using a CPU with the memory management module built-in.
It has bad bandwidth, though..

>Unless of course its the processor or cache doing it.. anyone know
>how to tell?

I don't..



Cheers,

