From: "Dave Edsall - The Tauminator" <edsall@iastate.edu>





   Many thanks to Steve Schweda, Rob Weirick , Richard Tibbets (from a post to 
this list I found at http://archive.redhat.com/axp-list/1998-July/0714.html) 
and to NekoTech (http://www.alphapower.com/Support/Appnotes/appnote-linux.htm) 
for the help in understanding what I needed to do to use SRM to boot from the 
hard disk. It worked like a charm!

   As for using console, I solved that problem as well. I cannot do a headless 
install but I now can do a headless boot. I needed to do the following:

   1) In SRM: 

      >>> set console serial

   2) Modify /etc/inittab to recognize a 9600 baud serial connection and 
modify
       /etc/securetty so that root could login via that connection.
   

   Once this was done, I was able to remove the keyboard, mouse and monitor, 
place the system in a rack and access it via our console server.

   Other annoyances I noticed after installation:


    1) The man pages would give a segmentation fault. I traced this to the man 
        configuration file.:

# Certain versions of the FHS recommend putting formatted versions of
# /usr/foopath/share/man/[locale/]manx/page.x into
# /var/cache/man/foopath/[locale/]catx/page.x.
# The keyword FHS will cause this behaviour (and overrides FSSTND).
# Explicitly given catdirs override.
#
# FSSTND
# FHS

   As I don't have formatted versions of the man pages installed, I 
commented-out these lines and that cured the problem.


   2) The machine appears to have a problem with the time. It either thinks it 
is 1910 or 2046. I installed NTP and ntpdate does not appear to work, giving 
all sorts of "poll"ing errors. I have since seen that this is a known problem 
with the kernel (2.0.34) and that I need to recompile a new kernel.



      Once again, many thanks!


          Dave






