A rising tide sinks all boats. Oh, wait....

> > Just a quick thought, there is a quote "...it will have FX32 embedded
> > into its kernel...".
> 
> Sounds like an NT thing. We already have as much access to FX!32 as
> we're gonna get via em86.

Are there any reassembler projects in the GNU domain?  I realize that this
type of project would not be easy, and multiple platform support would be
difficult, but I'm starting to see an increasing need for such a project. 
While Linux is becoming more popular, companies are favoring Intel's to
release their software, even though the true hiperf goes to the RISC
systems.  Which makes me curious why Oracle, Novell is only supporting
Intel.  I get the impression that Alpha, Sparc and PPC are the three
runners up in hardware popularity, so multiplatform vendors tend to ignore
at least one.

I wish we could convince these companies to Open Source, but I doubt they
will in the next 5-10 years, if ever.  Even Netscape only released their
brouser code.  The still have the hold on their web server stuff.  So now
that we established that for the near future, there will be a desire to run
binaries on other platforms that aren't native.

What would be a good way to do this?  I believe em86 is licenced only for
Alpha systems, and it doesn't translate binaries, so that's out.  Should
such a task start out translating iELF or emulating?  I suppose to get a
good handle on the myriad innards of ELF, a C program written to "execute"
a non-native ELF bin would be a good start.  This would be very
inefficient, but most portable, that way any Linux architecture can run it.
 Start with Intel, then do Alpha, Sparc and PPC, so all four systems could
exchange and use any binary.  Seems that RISC to RISC would be easier and
more efficient to emulate / translate.... but marginally less useful.

I realize that this is a daunting task, but I think the challenge is worth
the task.  Ideally, this type of program wouldn't be needed, but I'm seeing
more and more binaries without available source.  I probably couln't work
extensively on this program.  I don't know enough about Linux internals,
and I'd need to have tall stacks of data sheets.  Both concievably require
money for books too.  I've done some assembly - less than 8k size in 8086
code, so I may not know the depth of the pile of do-do that this project
may involve.

Thanks for your time.

Jeff.


