On Thu, 20 Aug 1998, Street Dreams wrote:
> I realize that this is a daunting task, but I think the challenge is worth
> the task.  Ideally, this type of program wouldn't be needed, but I'm seeing
> more and more binaries without available source.  I probably couln't work
> extensively on this program.  I don't know enough about Linux internals,
> and I'd need to have tall stacks of data sheets.  Both concievably require
> money for books too.  I've done some assembly - less than 8k size in 8086
> code, so I may not know the depth of the pile of do-do that this project
> may involve.

It is a daunting task, indeed.. and IMO, is not worth the man-power.
Reassemblers will be not be easier than a good emulator, as far as I can
see.. getting rock-solid emulation is a damn hard thing. Look at the
Dosemu project. Loads of manpower go into that project, and there are 
still bugs (not to take anything away from the Dosemu team. I've done
emulation work: I don't envy them). We are talking an Alpha emulator,
at some level, a Sparc emulator and a PPC emulator. Plus Intel. Getting
a batch of emulators like that working will be a pain. Reassembly will
be the only real choice because if we make it portable, speed will go
out the window. 

What we need is to support Java. We need Sparc, Alpha, PPC and Intel guys
to demand for Java-based apps. Java will perform well for office-suite
type software, and loads of other apps. For something like CAD, you 
might have to look at native code options if you are doing 3D rendering;
but for lots of stuff, Java looks good. Even if you eat a little bit
in performance, I for one would gladly take this loss to have the same
software running on multiple platforms in a clean, coherent way. 

Java rules. There was an old BASIC-type language for the OS-9 realtime
OS running on Motorola 6809 (and 68k, and now PPC) processors. It used an
intermediate-code type set up, similar to Java. I used a 6809 box for
years doing small scale control of stepper motors and stuff .. yeah,
I playing with robotics, what of it? :) .. anyhow, back then even, I
had the idea of making a language that used that i-code setup with
the runtime modules on various platforms. My friends and I just never
had the time to get much code off the ground.. its a big project..
anyhow Sun did a better job than I could anyhow.

Sun showed that the idea works; they gave us the Holy Grail of computing,
We just have to start using it for more than interactive/animated banners
on porn sites, for crying out loud.

Michael J. Graffam (mgraffam@mhv.net)
