On Sun, 23 Aug 1998, Gerardo Fdez. Navarrete wrote:

> - I have a Matrox Millenium II with 4 Mb (on a 64 bits slot :)), i only can get 256 colours, the X server drops a
> "screens not found" when i try more than 256 colours...

	Sounds like it's using it as a standard SVGA card, not treating it
as a Matrox Millenium.  I'd lay fault at the feet of the XF86Config file
until given reason to believe it belongs elsewhere.  I can send you mine
to look at, but using it with low-end monitors will likely do bad things
to the screen.

> - I tried to get the DEC DC21142 ethernet to work, i tried to put the "-DTULIP_PORT" (when i used tulip driver) and
> "eth0:fdx autosense=TP" when i used the generic DEC driver... I think the reason could be on the messages the kernel
> drops me on boot, the file dmesg show all thouse msg...

	And what do those messages say?
	The source I looked at for kernel 2.1.115 doesn't seem to have
TULIP_PORT in there anywhere; support for fixing the port is still there,
but the define is now as 2:  TULIP_DEFAULT_MEDIA_SWITCH should be defined
as the number of the media type you'd like (1 is 10b2, 2 is AUI, etc.),
and TULIP_NO_MEDIA_SWITCH prevents the driver from changing the media
type.

> - I tried to compile kernels from 2.1.115-2.1.117 but it's always fails on the same point, the mm directory... 

	This is a known bug in the egcs hiafa scheduler.  Hunt down an
egcs w/o hiafa (or with both the hiafa and the old scheduler; then tell it
to use the old) or just use gcc instead.  I've found gcc-2.8.1 to work
quite well on my machine.  egcs still seems faster, but I build kernels
more than I build anything else, and the frustration isn't worth it.  I
believe there are rpms of egcs w/o hiafa floating around, but I don't know
where.

- Bob



