Matthew Woodworth wrote:
> 
> I've been at this for days and I'm going bonkers!  In plain easy to
> understand terms, can anybody tell me how to create a valid floppy with
> milo on it?

Assuming that you are booting from ARC console or derivates (you do not
say) - take a dos formatted floppy (they are often sold as preformatted
and this is fine), put it in any machine which is able to write to such
floppies and copy on this floppy:
  - linload.exe
  - 'milo' for your platform and name this file 'milo'
  - a gzipped kernel you want to boot (name immaterial but assume
    that you called it 'vmlinux.gz').

That is about it.  Once you started 'linload.exe' as your OSLOADER
it will load milo.  At milo prompt type:

  boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

and you should be in business.  If you want to avoid filesystem
type guessing insert '-t msdos' after 'boot'.  Now, this will ask
you for a floppy with your (gzippped image of) ramdisk.  Have one
handy.

> Under 5.0 you just rawrite'd the image to a floppy.  That
> doesn't seem to work anymore.

Eh?  Probably you are writing a wrong image or your diskette has
bad sectors.  The later happens more often than you think.

  Michal


