Quoting Wes Bauske (wsb@paralleldata.com):
> That's an interesting observation. My systems have relatively
> light load for active processes but very heavy FP load on the
> main tests I run so they use few FD's. Also, my boxes sit behind
> a masquerading/passthru ISDN box so it's unlikely a port scan
> would occur on them. Maybe the people having problems are right
> out on the net where their systems can be portscanned/attacked
> in general?? If so, then this problem could stem from attacks
> by hackers??

Could be.  My box is the primary A (and MX) record for xcski.com.  I haven't
been able to run syslog for months because of this damn problem, so I don't
even know any more if I'm being portscanned.  I do know that when I was
running syslogd I was seeing two or three attempts to connect to my NNTP
server from people who weren't authorized, as well as the usual spammers
attempting to use my sendmail as a relay.  I also know that currently (not
running syslog) that every time I check, my file-nr is around 450.  But I also
run several mailing lists, and there have been times when I've had 20 or more
sendmail processes each attempting to send messages to 50 or more people,
making connections, so maybe that would trigger it, especially if the sockets
were slow to close.

But I haven't rebooted in 29 days and I'm reluctant to do any experiments with
logging turned on.

