> Exactly.. which what I wrote in my e-mail 600 ARE the fastest.  What I
> did say is that there is no 700 right now.  It annoys me when compose
> advertise the 700 MHz chips without telling people that they are just
> clock up 600 chips.  

I hate to break it to you, but this is standard procedure and there's
nothing wrong with it.

One thing most folks don't realize is that advanced semiconductor design
these days is as much a black art as it is a science.  Part of this is
because microprocessor designs themselves are so incredibly complicated
there's plenty of room for unanticipated interactions.  Another part of
this is the simple fact that at sub-micron geometries, quantum effects
start to dominate and the uncertainty principle comes into play 8-)
Semiconductor designers don't go around saying "Let's design a 500MHz
chip, then we'll design the 600MHz chip".  Instead, because of the
tremendous uncertainties involved they say "Let's design a chip that
has a reasonable chance of running reliably at a minimum of 500MHz".

So they design this chip and they start fabbing it.  When the chips start
rolling out of the fab two things happen.  The first is called "binning";
the chips are tested to see what speeds they'll run reliably at.  For
parts with a design center of 500MHz, the testing may start at 266 or 333
and go up to 700.  If you have a chip that runs reliably at 333 but not
at 500, it's better to sell it at a discount as a "slower" chip than to
scrap it entirely.  That's *why* semiconductor manufacturers sell CPUs at
a variety of speeds at different prices.

The second thing that happens is process improvemnt.  As a manufacturer
gains experience building a particular chip, they "tune" the process so as
to generate higher yields of faster chips.  Eventually they get to the point
where they start yielding higher speed levels in marketable quantities,
which is when they announce a new, faster chip 8-)

[this is a gross oversimplification; there are other things that 
chip makers can do to speed up chips, such as die-shrinks and the like,
but you get the idea]

The point being that you're not getting ripped off when you buy a chip
from the manufacturer that's listed at a higher speed than the original
design.  They wouldn't sell the chip at that speed unless they were
confident that it could perform at that speed.

