I hear you Jim, BUT:
At a certain point in clock speeds power consumption strts to ramp up very
quickly, which also translates into heat issues.

In the case of the 21164A this is significant.
Current CPUs of this family are being made with a .35 micron process, and
this places a real practical limit on speed because of power and heat.
Samsung IS making a 666MHz chip, but it has an added power regulation
package mounted on the chip. Even with this it is reputed to run quite hot.
Overclocking it ifurther is a fairly risky business. While not imposssible,
I think it is important to note that the "warranty" being provided on an
overclock is from the vendor, NOT the manufacturer.

Hopefully it will work, and usually overclocking Alpha CPUs is OK, as long
as proper attention is paid to providing enough power and cooling. In this
case, I wonder if we are not getting TOO close to "the edge".


