> doesn't seem to have anything regarding serial port troubles.

I used to use a Bitsurfr with my Multia/UDB with no problems. This was some
time ago using the first Craftworks Alpha release (I no longer use the UDB or
Craftworks).

A couple of things to watch out for, if you are not using a "Y" cable you will
have problems because the pins for the second port will confuse the syncronous
pins on the Bitsurfr, you need the "Y" even if you are using only one port.
Last I knew you could get the "Y" cable from Starship Computer Guys for less
than $20, or pay around $75 to get one from DEC.

Also, if you are using the Bitsurfr on port A, Milo and the kernel will send
messages to /dev/ttyS0 which may confuse the modem (you can disable this
behaviour but the default setup will do this). The solution is either to use
part B of turn off the Bitsurfr 'till after you have booted.


