From K6 vs. XL266 thread:

> > Exactly.. which what I wrote in my e-mail 600 ARE the fastest.  What I
> > did say is that there is no 700 right now.  It annoys me when compose
> > advertise the 700 MHz chips without telling people that they are just
> > clock up 600 chips.  

> So they design this chip and they start fabbing it.  When the chips start
> rolling out of the fab two things happen.  The first is called "binning";
> the chips are tested to see what speeds they'll run reliably at.  For
> parts with a design center of 500MHz, the testing may start at 266 or 333
> and go up to 700.  If you have a chip that runs reliably at 333 but not
> at 500, it's better to sell it at a discount as a "slower" chip than to
> scrap it entirely.  That's *why* semiconductor manufacturers sell CPUs at
> a variety of speeds at different prices.

> The point being that you're not getting ripped off when you buy a chip
> from the manufacturer that's listed at a higher speed than the original
> design.  They wouldn't sell the chip at that speed unless they were
> confident that it could perform at that speed.

I believe that the original comment was pointed at those who say it's a
700MHz unit when it was only factory rated to run at 600 or so.

I've known people that overclocked Intels and AMD's to varying results. 
Sometimes they overdid it so it wasn't overclockable.  Most times a 10%
clock rate increase can be achieved without any detectable problems.

Now, I am wary of overclocking, but it stands to reason that the 21164A
chips will drop in price soon when Gen 3 Alpha comes out.  So if I find a
600 chip cheap, I want to be able to switch from my 500.  Is there a way to
find the what dip switch settings on the main board signify?  I can't tell
how to get it.  I even found an on-line maintainance manual that has lots
of architectural and repair/replace information, but the settings aren't
called out.  I'm assuming that the boards aren't hard wired somewhere.  I
think even that manual calls them clock selectors or something.

psst... has anyone else noticed the pin-holes and chip pads for USB ports? 
They're just below the standard serial ports on the board.  Too bad they're
not supported by any Alpha OS yet.  Does anyone have a clue what hardware
hacking is needed to enable the physical port?  I imagine that now iMac and
Win98 have USB, more such hardware and software will become available.  I
think it'd be slick to solder the port and chip and go.

Thanks.


