No bogomips are not accurate, bogo meaning fake...ie fakemips.  One thing
to remember is the alpha is 64bits wide, can do up to 4 instructions per
clock...which 4/clock is normal you say...well yes but it uses a 128 or
256 bit buss to the memory buss...meaning it has little or no bottleneck
at the memory subsystem.

We had a mail server with K6-2 300, 128M SDRAM, 4 gig ultra wide scsi
(scsi 3) on the onboard wide scsi ...it kept dying from too much load and
the load average was up to 2.00 (ie I needed two processors).

We bought (actually I bought one for home, then the isp bought 4 of them),
the Digital XL233, the little Alphastation 200 that is the was at
onsale.com for $349 each.  We put 128M 60ns parity ram, 4 gig Fast SCSI II
on the onboard controller...this should be slower than the other system
scsi wise also.

Low an behold, its been running great, no downtime and the load is
load average: 0.39, 0.16, 0.10

well this is about the peak load it has, 
I see it like this more often than not
load average: 0.08, 0.07, 0.08

same amount of mail coming in...
that in laymans terms, means it plain kicks the intel-like processors
behind.  While my home intel machine is a Dual Pent Pro 200, with 128M
also...my alpha at home seems to beat it in certain tasks also...while at
others they are pretty much the same, and others that support the SMP,
allow the PPro to beat it...I could try some valid benches here...but I
have not had the desire to yet.

later
randal




