Adrian Gartland wrote:
> 
> On Wed, 26 Aug 1998, furio ercolessi wrote:
> 
> > Did anyone ever managed to get a stable kernel on a Ruffian?
> > If yes, please tell me how you did it!
.....
> 
> Uninstall egcs and stick on gcc 2.8.x or 2.7.x.  
> compile - install - reboot - enjoy

From my experience this is a very doubtful advice - especially
if you are talking about gcc 2.8.0 (which is really bad).  Most
of an advice you hear about not using egcs concerns Intel 2.0.x
kernels which use some **assembler** constructs which may confuse
optimizer.  For that you are supposed to use some very specific
gcc versions, which were used in a kernel development in the first
place, and definitely not 2.8.x.

Problems caused by "register pressure" are not likely to hit
you on Alpha and this thing is a bug with a long history which is
equally present at this moment in gcc and egcs.

General claims without supporting evidence are just that.

  Michal

p.s. Posting a bunch of registers dumps from **some** kernel is not
of a very great help.  Nobody but a poster can tie them up, with
a help of a System.map for this particular kernel, to a specific
piece of code.  Once that done it is not even very hard to
disassemble an associated machine ops with a help of objdump or gdb.
Without that this is just a pile of numbers.


