Hello all,

My 200 is turning out to be quite the groovy workstation at 
home.  I have a question about setting up the boot process.

I've pored over the addendum and various web pages.  There 
are many different ways to set it up.  My machine goes to the
SRM console at boot.  I must boot Milo from a floppy, then the
kernel from the HD, both at the command line.

I don't even care to see NT-ARC.  It seems that I can flash Milo
into ROM with environment variables, that will accomplish what
I need.  I'd like to be able to call my daughter at home, and
have her just type 'reboot' if a problem ever occurs, and having
it boot straight into Linux would be the best solution.
Just looking for verification here I guess.

So if I:
set MEMORY_SIZE 80
set BOOT_DEV sda1
set BOOT_FILE vmlinux.gz
set BOOT_STRING boot sda1:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda1
set AUTOBOOT (yes or true?)
set AUTOBOOT_TIMEOUT 30
bootopt 131 (assuming this is the correct address of Milo in ROM)

Then run the flash-management util:
>list (just to see where Milo is)
>program (and verify that the correct flash blocks will be overwritten)

Will Milo auto load linux every reboot and powerup?
(Of course this means I have to reflash ROM with every kernel upgrade.
Or maybe I could just rename the old, and replace it with the new...)

Anyway,
hopefully someone out there has accomplished this very thing.
Thank you all.

