Aaron Clow <AaronC@cmj.com> writes:
> 	Thanks to the help of many on this list, I think I see some
> light at the end of the very long, very dark tunnel named Jensen. I'm
> now at the point in RedHat installation with the header of Kernel where
> it's asking me to "Please insert your boot disk in the first disk drive
> if it's not already present." So I've tried the diskette with the
> pseudo-bootdisk jensen image and get "mount failed : invalid argument."
> I also tried the disk image (vmlinux.gz) I copied onto the root
> partition (hell, I'll try anything at this point). No dice. 

Well, the pseudo bootdisk should work (you should use the jensen.img
from the RH-CD), but if it doesn't, it won't hurt much. AFIK it's the
last step of the installation anyway. So you could try to boot your 
installed system using the same kernel as for the installation, but
now using '... root=/dev/sda2' (or similar) instead of 
'... root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1'

After booting, you should copy the jensen kernel manually to you root
partition, and then you can install your aboot.conf file in /etc
according to the docs.

HTH, Martin
