> It is from linux/drivers/block/genhd.c and traps happen on lines
> marked with '---->':
>
> 329				for (i = 0; i < 4 ; i++) {
> 330					struct partition *q = &p[i];
> 331					if (NR_SECTS(q)
> 332				----->	   && (q->sector & 63) == 1
> 333				----->	   && (q->end_sector & 63) == 63) {
>
> sizeof(struct partition) appears to be 16 so maybe I miss something
> but it seems a bit of strange that I see only two pairs of traps and
> not four.

Maybe you only have two actual partitions on that disk?

The traps occur because p points to the partition table portion of a
disk sector.  The disk sector is properly aligned, but the partition
table (at offset 0xFBE by IBM's/Microsoft's design) is not properly
aligned within the sector.

The fix for the traps is to have those marked lines use get_aligned the
way the line immediately above them does, as explained at the top of
genhd.c:

	/*
	 * Many architectures don't like unaligned accesses, which is
	 * frequently the case with the nr_sects and start_sect partition
	 * table entries.
	 */
	#include <asm/unaligned.h>

	#define SYS_IND(p)	get_unaligned(&p->sys_ind)
	#define NR_SECTS(p)	get_unaligned(&p->nr_sects)
	#define START_SECT(p)	get_unaligned(&p->start_sect)

Add two similar macros for sector and end_sector and use them.

